{:edb/db-before-t 1416 :edb/db-after-t 1417 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534729 50 #inst "2026-10-08T05:16:15.542Z" 13194139534729 true] [17592186096545 1020 #edb/ref 17592186306071 13194139534729 true] [17592186096546 1020 #edb/ref 17592186306057 13194139534729 true] [17592186096547 1020 #edb/ref 17592186306050 13194139534729 true] [17592186096547 1020 #edb/ref 17592186306064 13194139534729 true] [17592186097137 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/assets/19e9763d62ca7818024bf49522a127a48dd04f76d1175c93e060a1043b6a5795.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?" 13194139534729 false] [17592186097137 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040224/assets/19e9763d62ca7818024bf49522a127a48dd04f76d1175c93e060a1043b6a5795.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?" 13194139534729 true] [17592186306050 1017 #edb/ref 17592186306051 13194139534729 true] [17592186306050 1089 "q-21035" 13194139534729 true] [17592186306050 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534729 true] [17592186306050 1100 "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)=12x-7+\\frac{3}{x}.$" 13194139534729 true] [17592186306050 1113 #uuid "80fd1b01-b4c3-5a42-879f-a9a61102a17e" 13194139534729 true] [17592186306050 1145 "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =12-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}}=0\n$$\n\nThe equation above has no real solutions, so there are no points of inflection." 13194139534729 true] [17592186306051 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306052 13194139534729 true] [17592186306051 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306053 13194139534729 true] [17592186306051 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306054 13194139534729 true] [17592186306051 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306055 13194139534729 true] [17592186306051 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306056 13194139534729 true] [17592186306051 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534729 true] [17592186306051 1041 "selection" 13194139534729 true] [17592186306051 1104 #edb/ref 17592186306055 13194139534729 true] [17592186306051 1110 #uuid "bc031990-69d2-56b3-825b-b891ab692887" 13194139534729 true] [17592186306052 1034 "x=3" 13194139534729 true] [17592186306052 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534729 true] [17592186306052 1111 #uuid "adef789f-225b-5894-a8b9-5e11934c377c" 13194139534729 true] [17592186306053 1034 "x=2" 13194139534729 true] [17592186306053 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534729 true] [17592186306053 1111 #uuid "ee1fd109-16af-5879-bcfd-4d1366864d1f" 13194139534729 true] [17592186306054 1034 "x=4" 13194139534729 true] [17592186306054 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534729 true] [17592186306054 1111 #uuid "0cd4ab4f-95a1-5b0c-80a0-5c09c687f7aa" 13194139534729 true] [17592186306055 1034 "There are no points of inflection" 13194139534729 true] [17592186306055 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534729 true] [17592186306055 1111 #uuid "abf597b9-e766-597c-b9d7-6faf4a80df03" 13194139534729 true] [17592186306056 1034 "x=-4" 13194139534729 true] [17592186306056 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534729 true] [17592186306056 1111 #uuid "6d418e2c-fc04-5c80-b390-b1fd8c7464a9" 13194139534729 true] [17592186306057 1017 #edb/ref 17592186306058 13194139534729 true] [17592186306057 1089 "q-21041" 13194139534729 true] [17592186306057 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534729 true] [17592186306057 1100 "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)=3x-7+\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.$" 13194139534729 true] [17592186306057 1113 #uuid "51c9af9b-7fc6-5955-bfbb-b82d2cff1bc7" 13194139534729 true] [17592186306057 1145 "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, notice that $f(x)$ is not defined at $x=0,$ so this can't be a point of inflection.\n\nNow, we calculate the second derivative: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}(1-\\frac{3}{x}) & =0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{3}{x} & =0 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=3.$ So, we pick two points around it, say, $x=2$ and $x=4$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(2) & =\\frac{2}{(2)^{3}}-\\frac{6}{(2)^{4}}=-\\frac{1}{8}<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(4) & =\\frac{2}{(4)^{3}}-\\frac{6}{(4)^{4}}=\\frac{1}{128}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=3,$ we conclude that $x=3$ is indeed a point of inflection." 13194139534729 true] [17592186306058 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306059 13194139534729 true] [17592186306058 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306060 13194139534729 true] [17592186306058 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306061 13194139534729 true] [17592186306058 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306062 13194139534729 true] [17592186306058 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306063 13194139534729 true] [17592186306058 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534729 true] [17592186306058 1041 "selection" 13194139534729 true] [17592186306058 1104 #edb/ref 17592186306059 13194139534729 true] [17592186306058 1