6717.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y={g}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $g(x)$ have a point of inflection?" 13194139534729 true] [17592186306071 1113 #uuid "195b2308-1c8c-58f1-bee1-408b56e897dd" 13194139534729 true] [17592186306071 1145 "First, notice that since $g(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${g}^{″}(x)$ must switch signs. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${g}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={g}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis only at at $x=1.$ \n\nTherefore, $g(x)$ has point of inflection at $x=1.$" 13194139534729 true] [17592186306072 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306073 13194139534729 true] [17592186306072 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306074 13194139534729 true] [17592186306072 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306075 13194139534729 true] [17592186306072 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306076 13194139534729 true] [17592186306072 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306077 13194139534729 true] [17592186306072 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534729 true] [17592186306072 1041 "selection" 13194139534729 true] [17592186306072 1104 #edb/ref 17592186306073 13194139534729 true] [17592186306072 1110 #uuid "1cc558ef-5644-5852-a40b-9f436fa85c52" 13194139534729 true] [17592186306073 1034 "x=1" 13194139534729 true] [17592186306073 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534729 true] [17592186306073 1111 #uuid "0fd912d0-af60-5de5-917e-56811ac061fc" 13194139534729 true] [17592186306074 1034 "x=-3" 13194139534729 true] [17592186306074 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534729 true] [17592186306074 1111 #uuid "d5da1f00-098a-58e6-96f8-9b08133db7e2" 13194139534729 true] [17592186306075 1034 "$x=-3,$ $x=1,$ and $x=2$" 13194139534729 true] [17592186306075 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534729 true] [1759218630